    "projection": "Equirectangular Carrington longitude increasing right, north up, longitude 0 at the left seam. Fourteen near-simultaneous pairs sampled every two days. Inverse perspective uses each north-up FITS WCS disk center, radius, observer latitude, Carrington longitude and observer distance. Measured center-to-limb quiet intensity is removed for each date. Narrow central-meridian strips are feathered; grazing and polar regions taper to quiet mean intensity.",
    "channelsInDerivedImage": {
      "R": "Local HMI continuum intensity contrast",
      "G": "Log intensity contrast of the AIA 170 nm bright network",
      "B": "HMI sunspot groups. Small footprints are enlarged locally to remain legible on the map."
    },
    "rendering": "Sphere sampled for the current observer and simulation day using the IAU north pole and a rigid sidereal rotation of 14.1844 degrees/day. The artwork combines different dates. Simulated warm-white exposure and limb darkening do not reproduce a single acquisition or activity at the simulated date.",
    "rotationReference": "https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/pck/pck00011.tpc"
  },
  "innerCorona": {
    "credit": "Courtesy of NASA/SDO and the AIA, EVE, and HMI science teams.",
    "sources": "corona-sequence-credits.json",
    "observationUTC": [
      "2024-05-10T08:46:10Z",
      "2024-05-11T07:47:10Z"
    ],
    "processing": "24 actual north-up AIA 17.1 nm frames. Reprojected from the registered optical-corona sequence, reducing the previously enlarged height by a factor of 0.68 (about 1.60 times observed height). Pearl-gold color represents EUV intensity. Four frames/second independent of the simulation clock.",
    "layout": "6 by 4 tiles, each 256 px square, disk radius 0.32 of a tile width."
  },
  "outerCorona": {
    "credit": "NASA/Keegan Barber",
    "date": "2024-04-08",
    "place": "Dallas, Texas",
    "article": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/centers-and-facilities/goddard/2024-total-solar-eclipse-prediction-vs-reality/",
    "source": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/2024-actual-keegan-barber-v2.png",
    "sha256": "5fc624d97070b7494843fe0be6d010e70de06efa3d1de0565013947495b75aee",
    "processing": "Only the actual eclipse photograph is used. A circle fit locates the lunar limb. The surrounding white-light field was resampled out to 2.48 disk radii, background-subtracted, tapered and mapped to translucent warm-white exposure. This archival projection does not reconstruct the near and far sides of the corona."
  },
